SPACED — WEDGE (CANONICAL)

Primary Wedge
Need a space for a while? Book a private room or a whole place near you in minutes.

Secondary Variant
A room, an apartment, a house — yours for as long as you need it.

Core Moment
A user needs a private space for a short time, nearby, without a lease,
a booking window, or an explanation.

Scope
Residential spaces only.

- a whole apartment
- a whole house
- a studio
- a room inside one of these — bedroom, home office, kitchen

Discretion is supported, not required. It is one reason among several a
person needs a space for an hour, and the product does not assume or ask
which reason applies.

What This Is
A wedge.
Not the MVP.
Not the feature list.
Not the operational spec.

Purpose
Use this wedge to align:
- field testing messages
- CTA language
- first-screen copy
- product direction

Do Not
- mention specific durations in the wedge
- mention 45–60 minutes
- over-explain the concept
- broaden it beyond residential space
- broaden it into a generic marketplace for space
- dilute urgency
- frame the product around any single use case

Notes
Duration flexibility belongs in the MVP and booking flow, not in the wedge.
The wedge must stay broad enough to pull interest, but sharp enough to
communicate immediate need.

The residential boundary is the load-bearing constraint. Commercial space
(offices, conference rooms, commercial kitchens) is a different market with
different users and different intent — it is gated out of V1 by config, not
excluded from the schema. See docs/technical/SCHEMA.md §4.1.

Status
REVISED — 2026-07-28
Superseded the original wedge, which was narrower on two axes: private rooms
only, and discretion as the defining use case. Both were widened deliberately:
the space types now span whole residences and the rooms within them, and
discretion became one use case among several rather than the frame.

What did NOT change: residential only, near you, short duration, minutes to
book, no lease or commitment.

Change this file only if real-world testing clearly proves the wedge is weak.

